            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "a372974f-4cea-4702-852d-40e70e155583",
               "text": "Wheelock, F. (ed. R. LaFleur)",
               "page": "https://www.csun.edu/~hcfll004/latin-numbers.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2005",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Next Numbers\n\nWith Mesoamerican numbers, we saw that the bar (5) was kept and more units were added on top to form the next four numbers. The same logic appears in Etruscan-Roman numbers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"missing-numbers-table-2\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Table 2: Comparison of Etruscan-Roman, Hindu-Arabic, and Mesoamerican numbers from 5 to 9.*",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "f403c4d1-e1f0-40a7-a19f-d0cabf2d7313",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "f403c4d1-e1f0-40a7-a19f-d0cabf2d7313",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Let Us Think!\n\nThe logic behind the first numbers is the same for Etruscan-Roman and Mesoamerican systems. Does this mean one people borrowed the idea from the other?",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4d2971d9-d6d6-4e7f-9f49-d2ee220d6d2e",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "no",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4d2971d9-d6d6-4e7f-9f49-d2ee220d6d2e",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Lack of Evidence\n\nThis question raises the idea of connections between peoples — an idea we already dismissed in a previous class.\n\nNumbers being built the same way does not mean the peoples were in contact.\n\nBesides having different formats, the logic for writing Etruscan-Roman numbers changes for large numbers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4d9543db-8997-4f15-bf3e-4ceafe3ee925",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4d9543db-8997-4f15-bf3e-4ceafe3ee925",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Number 10\n\nAs we saw before, the number 10 (X) was already the basis for the number 5 (V). Mesoamerican numbers, on the other hand, continue with the same logic used for numbers 1 to 5.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"missing-numbers-ten\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "27d5eb4b-f9a9-4816-b211-ab7c94aaecfc",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "27d5eb4b-f9a9-4816-b211-ab7c94aaecfc",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned that Etruscan-Roman numbers followed a logic similar to Mesoamerican numbers, but there is no evidence that the peoples borrowed number systems from each other — even though they were contemporaneous.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
